Z||000007||Oreif||11-03-2001||02:01 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B>I used to like them, as my uncle retired from Grumman (practically invented ECM), but it's too bad the greedy traitors gouged prices.  It was in the news the other day.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, it was "alledged" by an employee that was fired for stealing back in 1988 and an auditor who claimed he saw it happen everyday. Both employees worked for only a few years and both were fired. All of the "alledged" things happened 13 years ago. It's just that it is finally going to court now. With all the spot-lights on the defense industry, the media thinks 13 year old news is relevant. Back then it wasn't even front page news. There are many things now that prevent anything like they alledge from happening.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||11-03-2001||07:03 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Roger, Where does she work??? (State)<BR>I work at the Illinois Northrop/Grumman plant.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>She works for E Systems, they do contract electronic systems for Grumman,Lockeed, Boeing. Shes the editor/publisher of manuals,<BR>newsletteres, etc. Just outside of Dallas in Greenville tx.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cheever3000||11-03-2001||10:25 AM||||Oreif: Thanks for the correction.  I feel better now.  My uncle did not invent their electronic stuff, but rather made it work when the engineers couldn't figure out what was wrong.  He mostly talked about the jamming pods, AWACS, E-A6's, F-14's and even something about gunboats they sold to Israel (I don't know what electronic goodies would be on a gunboat).  He's a civilian, but went on carrier tours sometimes.  Once he was going to talk with an F-14 test pilot after landing, but saw the guy's C.O. arguing with him and pointing at the plane. My uncle asked what that was all about, and the pilot showed him scratches on the bottom of the plane.  Seems he was buzzing Russian fishing boats 6 miles offshore, and their antennaes scratched the bottom of the plane!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000010||maryjane||11-03-2001||11:03 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR>He mostly talked about the jamming pods, AWACS, E-A6's, F-14's and even something about gunboats they sold to Israel (I don't know what electronic goodies would be on a gunboat).  He's a civilian, but went on carrier tours sometimes.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Cheeve, gunboats are small, fast, and leave small radar sigs. They are often stuffed with intell equip after delivery, and used for spying, and SOF insertion/extraction.<BR>A lot of people don't realize the number of civilian tech reps that are used with military units. Even in Viet Nam, we had 2 Sikorsky Helicopter factory reps with our squadron all the time I was over there. I suspect they command pretty hefty salaries for that type assignment.||||reg||1
